Author: Danny Milosavljevic <dannym@friendly-machines.com>
Date: 2026-04-20
Subject: Only treat the -1..-4095 return range as a syscall error
License: expat

FPC 1.0.6's generic Linux SysCall wrapper flags *every* negative
longint returned from `do_SysCall' as an error and converts it to
 -1 + errno.

That's wrong for pointer-returning syscalls: on i386,
mmap/mmap2/brk/mremap legitimately return addresses with the sign
bit set (anything above 2 GiB in the user-space half of the address
map), which this code misreports as a failure.  Modern Linux
kernels hand out exactly such addresses when the heap grows past
2 GiB or when the kernel's mmap-base lands high, so FPC programs
die at startup ("heap grow failed", "mmap failed") on any box that
isn't the 1999-era low-memory layout FPC 0.99.x/1.0.6 was written
for.

The Linux kernel's actual convention is that a syscall error is the
range [-4095, -1]; anything outside that is a valid return value.
This patch adds the lower bound.

--- a/rtl/linux/syscalls.inc
+++ b/rtl/linux/syscalls.inc
@@ -104,7 +104,11 @@
 }
 begin
   do_SysCall(callnr,regs);
-  if regs.reg1<0 then
+  { Linux reports syscall errors as -errno in the range -1..-4095.
+    Pointer-returning syscalls such as mmap can legitimately return
+    addresses with the sign bit set on i386, so treating every
+    negative longint as an error breaks heap growth on modern kernels. }
+  if (regs.reg1<0) and (regs.reg1>=-4095) then
    begin
 {$IFDEF SYSCALL_DEBUG}
      If DoSysCallDebug then
